rota lap 5172, row 2 -- Fire, the row that sees, cut and stop. Read foundations/20260816-214652_standfast-the-stopped-line.md, whose FOUNDING CASE is this very page corrupting itself in silence while every standing guard read past it, and whose sentence is a lantern that fires twice becomes a loom. The class had fired three times. The rota chose the lap.
think Opened on the card's directive and took stash@{0} -- the dead lap's whole set, which was stash@{9}'s work plus its log and account, parked by the round-open at 220223.
think Two conflicts. The ITINERARY one was the nib. The REDS one was a real collision: upstream had bound %789 to a peer's row while the parked row claimed the same number. The stamp is the key and the number is a view, so the unshared row renumbered -- three times before the send, as peers published 790, 792 and 794 underneath it.
think Reading the card to resolve it, found INNER LOOP item 7 absent at HEAD while the card and %789 both said CLOSED. Commit 00493b3be re-ate it at 214715, ten minutes after db4ae9f0b restored what 5f96df5e14 took that morning, and it stood absent through six further commits.
think Wrote a probe for the ordered-list class before building anything, to size the population. It answered zero on the broken card, which was wrong, and the wrongness is what found the second fault.
think Commit 99d4948e6 had wrapped a sentence so a bare triple-backtick fence opener landed at column 0 in prose. CommonMark runs an unclosed fence to end of document, so 30,791 of the card's 38,970 bytes rendered as one code block -- and the fence was swallowing every list below it, which is why the probe read zero.
think Claimed the class. The push collided seventeen seconds behind bakery-living-pin-list-spine, whose list reading is stricter -- no foreign line between two items catches the eaten directive where a split-at-restart reading does not. Yielded that half whole on the rebase and kept the fence.
think Resolving the autostash conflict the first time swallowed a peer's published %790 row. Caught before the commit by re-reading the row list against upstream, which is the %787 failure mode exactly.
think The final rebase found sixteen peer commits, among them bakery's landed list guard, the restoration of item 7, and folds of every row this lap had renumbered onto. Aborted rather than resolved: a ledger conflict across four files where both numbers and shelves had moved is a rewrite, not a merge. Reset to upstream, restored this lap's own files, and re-derived the row at 797 against the new spine.
think That abort is what made the lap honest. Item 7 was already restored upstream, so the only fault left standing was the fence, and this account claims exactly that.
obs The root cause fired a SECOND time on the same page within the hour, caught by bakery's sibling guard: a wrapped sentence left 32. at column 0, which CommonMark reads as a list item. Both faults are one habit -- a hand wrapping prose to a column creates markup it never typed, since a fence opener and a list marker are each decided by what stands at the start of a line.
obs A page can be byte-perfect and structurally broken. ascii_document counts characters, tracked_link and link_text_promise read links, the pin bound counts length, nib_honesty reads one key -- every one green throughout, every one reading bytes that had not changed.
obs The wall is at zero rather than a ratchet because exactly one unbalanced fence stood in 453 living pages. A population already at zero earns a wall.
obs The control's own first draft passed three mutations by copying the planted repository OVER the mutant scan, so every mutation ran the pristine reader. A mutation leg that passes because it measured the wrong program is worse than no leg.
obs The IFS mutation made the scan crash until an absent-path step was added, after which it undercounts in silence. The quieter failure is the more dangerous one, so the leg now asserts the silence.
obs First residency found the absent-path fault on this lap's own staged deletion, and the first repair counted it inside a command substitution, which is a subshell, so it reported zero absent paths on the very run that had been killed by one.
